# Confirmation Modal - Delete Item

**Vanilla JS + HTML:**

```html
<div id="deleteModal" class="modal hidden">
  <div class="modal-content">
    <p>Are you sure you want to delete this item?</p>
    <button id="confirmDelete">Delete</button>
    <button id="cancelDelete">Cancel</button>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .modal { display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; 
    position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; 
    background: rgba(0,0,0,0.5); }
  .modal.hidden { display: none; }
  .modal-content { background: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; }
</style>

<script>
  function deleteItem(id) {
    document.getElementById('deleteModal').classList.remove('hidden');
    document.getElementById('confirmDelete').onclick = () => {
      // Actually delete
      fetch(`/api/items/${id}`, { method: 'DELETE' });
      document.getElementById('deleteModal').classList.add('hidden');
    };
    document.getElementById('cancelDelete').onclick = () => {
      document.getElementById('deleteModal').classList.add('hidden');
    };
  }
</script>
```

**Or use the browser's native one-liner:**
```js
if (confirm('Delete this item?')) {
  fetch(`/api/items/${id}`, { method: 'DELETE' });
}
```

The native `confirm()` is simpler and works everywhere — go custom modal only if you need specific styling.

**React version** (if that's your stack):
```jsx
export function DeleteModal({ itemId, onConfirm, onCancel }) {
  return (
    <div className="modal">
      <div className="modal-content">
        <p>Are you sure?</p>
        <button onClick={() => onConfirm(itemId)}>Delete</button>
        <button onClick={onCancel}>Cancel</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Skipped: custom styling (add your own). Native confirm covers 95% of delete flows — go modal only if UX testing says so.
